{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2277 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5891e37120daaf84ce3734b8cf046eff1c116f1eba5ef1de8bff43ea2cbcd22c" :edb/db-before-t 2276 :edb/db-after-t 2277 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535589 50 "2026-10-14T16:40:25.422Z" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186064337 1038 17592186520476 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186064338 1038 17592186520447 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186064338 1038 17592186520482 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186077240 1078 17592186077242 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078199 1071 "Find the common ratio of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n90, \\: -30, \\: 10, \\: -\\dfrac{10}{3}, \\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078199 1071 "Find the common ratio of the following geometric sequence.\n$90, \\: -30, \\: 10, \\: -\\dfrac{10}{3}, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078199 1074 "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{(-30)}{90} = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078199 1074 "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{(-30)}{90} = -\\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078200 1071 "Find the $7$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1, \\: 2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078200 1071 "Find the $7$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n$1, \\: 2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078200 1074 "![](images/65/653f9ccfe28f5e3630afd30562064f1d790245b227896ea7db2da4265f7bac1a.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $2$.\n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $7$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/6a/6ae31d38f90d943b5bd802652d0dedd4925351b1ffadd6398d018a1dd4c7f0d3.png)\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is $64$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078200 1074 "![](images/65/653f9ccfe28f5e3630afd30562064f1d790245b227896ea7db2da4265f7bac1a.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $2.$\n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $7$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/6a/6ae31d38f90d943b5bd802652d0dedd4925351b1ffadd6398d018a1dd4c7f0d3.png)\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is $64.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078201 1071 "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\n1. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n2. $1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots$\n3. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078201 1071 "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots$\n\nIII. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078201 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{16}{8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(-\\frac{1}{3})} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{27})}{(\\frac{1}{9})} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078201 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n$\\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{4}{2}|= 2; \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = \\frac{8}{4}|= 2; \\frac{a_{4}}{a_{3}} = \\frac{16}{8}|= 2$\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n$\\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = ((-\\frac{1}{3}))/(1)|=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = ((\\frac{1}{9}))/((-\\frac{1}{3}))|=-\\frac{1}{3}; \\frac{a_{4}}{a_{3}} = ((-\\frac{1}{27}))/((\\frac{1}{9}))|=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n$\\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{8}{4}|= 2; \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = \\frac{12}{8}|= \\frac{3}{2}$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078214 1071 "Given that $x$, $3x$, $36$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078214 1071 "Given that $x$, $3x$, $36$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078214 1074 "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=x,\\,{a}_{2}=3x,\\,{a}_{3}=36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{36}{3x}=\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{12}{x} \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078214 1074 "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\na_{1} = x, a_{2} = 3x, a_{3} = 36\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{3x}{x} = 3\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{3}}{a_{2}} = \\frac{36}{3x} = \\frac{12}{x}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 &= \\frac{12}{x} \\\\\n3x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186078544 1038 17592186520471 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078544 1038 17592186520495 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078545 1038 17592186520518 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078546 1038 17592186520456 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078546 1038 17592186520490 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078547 1038 17592186520446 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078547 1038 17592186520470 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186078694 1050 540.0 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186079077 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186079077 1071 "Find the $5$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n2,\\,-4,\\,8,\\,-16,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186079077 1071 "Find the $5$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n$2,-4, 8,-16, …$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186079077 1074 "![](images/7c/7c6697d2a70a7499e6a834f3e588257c88f5da433c7365b9b1777314377e122d.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $-2.$\n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $5$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a5cd75c3e262f76d12da48cd8e86bf04b2532f7e7084d1968ac30bd103373f64.png)\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is $32.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186079625 1064 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\n1,\\quad\\:\\: 3,\\quad\\:\\: 9,\\quad\\:\\: 27,\\quad\\:\\: 81,\\quad\\:\\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that each term is ${\\color{blue}3}$ times the previous term:\n\n![](images/9a/9a3af8dd763aa0ab6b176a3a9d711ead4d029e2cebef823f906403be5fc38786.png)\n\nA sequence like the one above, where each term is found by multiplying the previous term by a fixed constant, is called a **geometric sequence**.\n\nThis fixed constant is called the **common ratio**, and is denoted by $r$. In this particular case, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr={\\color{blue}3}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186079625 1064 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\n1,\\quad\\:\\: 3,\\quad\\:\\: 9,\\quad\\:\\: 27,\\quad\\:\\: 81,\\quad\\:\\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that each term is ${\\color{blue}3}$ times the previous term:\n\n![](images/9a/9a3af8dd763aa0ab6b176a3a9d711ead4d029e2cebef823f906403be5fc38786.png)\n\nA sequence like the one above, where each term is found by multiplying the previous term by a fixed constant, is called a **geometric sequence**. \n\nThis fixed constant is called the **common ratio**, and is denoted by $r.$ In this particular case, the common ratio is $r={\\color{blue}3}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080087 1075 17592186080088 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080087 1075 17592186080089 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080087 1075 17592186080090 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080087 1075 17592186080091 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080087 1075 17592186080092 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080087 1075 17592186520024 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080087 1075 17592186520249 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080087 1075 17592186520254 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080087 1075 17592186520277 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080087 1075 17592186520317 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080087 1078 17592186520277 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080999 1075 17592186081000 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080999 1075 17592186081001 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080999 1075 17592186081002 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080999 1075 17592186081003 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080999 1075 17592186081004 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186080999 1075 17592186520013 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080999 1075 17592186520037 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080999 1075 17592186520081 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080999 1075 17592186520155 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080999 1075 17592186520238 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186080999 1078 17592186520013 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082985 1074 "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare $10^3 = 1\\,000$ with $10^x = a,$ we get that $a = 1\\,000$ and $x = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\log{1\\,000} = 3.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082985 1074 "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n10^3 = 1\\,000\n$$\n\nwith $10^x = a$, we get that $a = 1\\,000$ and $x = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\log{1\\,000} = 3\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082986 1071 "Evaluate $\\log\\left( 10^{7}\\right).$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082986 1071 "Evaluate $\\log\\left(10^{7}\\right)$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082986 1074 "The common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x =7$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^{7}\\right) = 7\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082986 1074 "The common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x =7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left( 10^{7}\\right) = 7.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082987 1071 "Evaluate $\\log\\left( 0.1 \\right).$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082987 1071 "Evaluate $\\log\\left(0.1 \\right)$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082987 1074 "First, we rewrite $0.1$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.1 = 10^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log\\left( 0.1 \\right) = \\log\\left(10^{-1}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left( 10^{-1} \\right)= -1.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082987 1074 "First, we rewrite $0.1$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.1 = 10^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(0.1 \\right) = \\log\\left(10^{-1}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^{-1} \\right)= -1\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082988 1071 "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $2\\log(7) + 5\\log(5)$?" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082988 1071 "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $2\\log(7) + 5\\log(5)?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186082988 1074 "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\log (7) + 5\\log (5) \\mid \\approx 2(0.84510) + 5(0.69897); = 1.69020 + 3.49485; = 5.18505; \\approx 5.19\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186082988 1074 "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)+5\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & ≈2(0.845\\,10)+5(0.698\\,97) \\\\  & =1.690\\,20+3.494\\,85 \\\\  & =5.185\\,05 \\\\  & ≈5.19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083042 1071 "Solve $2x^3+3x^2-8x-12=0$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083042 1071 "Solve $2x^3+3x^2-8x-12=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083042 1074 "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{3} + 3x^{2} - 8x - 12 &=  \\\\\n(2x^{3} + 3x^{2}) - (8x + 12) &=  \\\\\nx^{2}(2x + 3) - 4(2x + 3) &=  \\\\\n(2x + 3) \\cdot x^{2} - (2x + 3) \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n&= (2x + 3)(x^{2} - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(x^2-4) = 0\n$$\n\nby setting each factor equal to $0$.\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x+3)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$2x + 3|= 0; 2x|=-3; x|=-\\frac{3}{2}$.\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-4)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x^{2} - 4|= 0; x^{2}|= 4; x|= ± 2$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-2, \\; -\\dfrac{3}{2}, \\; 2\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083042 1074 "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-8x-12 & = \\\\ (2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})-(8x+12) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(2x+3)-4(2x+3) & = \\\\ (2x+3)\\cdot {x}^{2}-(2x+3)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ (2x+3)({x}^{2}-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2x+3)(x^2-4) = 0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-2, \\; -\\dfrac{3}{2}, \\; 2.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083043 1071 "Solve $8x^3+3=4x^2+6x$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083043 1071 "Solve $8x^3+3=4x^2+6x.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083043 1074 "First, we subtract $4x^2$ and $6x$ from both sides to make the right side $0$. To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n8x^3-4x^2-6x+3=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x^{3} - 4x^{2} - 6x + 3 &=  \\\\\n(8x^{3} - 4x^{2}) - (6x - 3) &=  \\\\\n4x^{2}(2x - 1) - 3(2x - 1) &=  \\\\\n(2x - 1) \\cdot 4x^{2} - (2x - 1) \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n&= (2x - 1)(4x^{2} - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$2x - 1|= 0; 2x|= 1; x|= \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- Setting the factor $(4x^2-3)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$4x^{2} - 3|= 0; 4x^{2}|= 3; x^{2}|= \\frac{3}{4}; x|= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}; x|= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}, \\; \\dfrac12, \\; \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083043 1074 "First, we subtract $4x^2$ and $6x$ from both sides to make the right side $0.$ To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n8x^3-4x^2-6x+3=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-6x+3 & = \\\\ (8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})-(6x-3) & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-1)-3(2x-1) & = \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot 4{x}^{2}-(2x-1)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (2x-1)(4{x}^{2}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(4x^2-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}, \\; \\dfrac12, \\; \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083067 1071 "Solve $x^3+x^2-9x-9=0$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083067 1071 "Solve $x^3+x^2-9x-9=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083067 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + x^{2} - 9x - 9 &=  \\\\\n(x^{3} + x^{2}) - (9x + 9) &=  \\\\\nx^{2}(x + 1) - 9(x + 1) &=  \\\\\n(x + 1) \\cdot x^{2} - (x + 1) \\cdot 9 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 1)(x^{2} - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x^2-9) = 0\n$$\n\nby setting each factor equal to $0$.\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+1)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x + 1|= 0; x|=-1$.\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-9)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x^{2} - 9|= 0; x^{2}|= 9; x|= ± 3$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\, \\pm 3\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083067 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x-9 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}+{x}^{2})-(9