$0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-4, \\pm \\sqrt 5.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083172 1038 17592186520513 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083173 1038 17592186520505 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083174 1038 17592186520450 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083174 1038 17592186520498 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083174 1038 17592186520511 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083175 1038 17592186520448 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083175 1038 17592186520469 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083246 1038 17592186520493 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083247 1038 17592186520461 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083247 1038 17592186520512 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083247 1038 17592186520517 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083274 1038 17592186520454 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083274 1038 17592186520459 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083274 1038 17592186520507 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083274 1038 17592186520515 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083275 1038 17592186520494 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083421 1050 420.0 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083422 1050 720.0 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083532 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083532 1071 "Solve $12y^{3} + 10y^{2} - 6y - 5 = 0$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083532 1071 "Solve $12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-6y-5=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083532 1074 "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-6y-5 & = \\\\ (12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2})-(6y+5) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(6y+5)-(6y+5) & = \\\\ (6y+5)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(6y+5)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (6y+5)(2{y}^{2}-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(6y+5)(2{y}^{2}-1)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(6y+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+5 & =0 \\\\ 6y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083678 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083678 1071 "Solve $-9y^{3} + 27y^{2} + y - 3 = 0$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186083678 1071 "Solve $-9{y}^{3}+27{y}^{2}+y-3=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186083678 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n9{y}^{3}-27{y}^{2}-y+3=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{3}-27{y}^{2}-y+3 & = \\\\ 9{y}^{2}(y-3)-1(y-3) & = \\\\ (9{y}^{2}-1)(y-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(9{y}^{2}-1)(y-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(y-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=3,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186084436 1064 "Suppose we are given the logarithm $\\log (100)$. If no base is specified, then by default the base is equal to $10$.\n\nIn our example, we have\n\n$$\n\\log(100)=\\log_{10}(100)=\\log_{10}(10^2) = 2\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm with base $10$ is called the **common logarithm**, and is defined as follows:\n\n> $a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nEverything we did with logarithms, we can do with the common logarithm. For example, we can\n\n- convert between exponential and logarithm form,\n- evaluate common logarithm expressions, and\n- calculate specific values of $\\log a$ using a calculator.\n\nFinally, note that most calculators have a button for the common logarithm. This is usually denoted $\\log_{10}$ or simply $\\log$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186084436 1064 "Suppose we are given the logarithm $\\log (100).$ If no base is specified, then by default the base is equal to $10.$\n\nIn our example, we have\n\n$$\n\\log(100)=\\log_{10}(100)=\\log_{10}(10^2) = 2.\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm with base $10$ is called the **common logarithm**, and is defined as follows:\n\n$a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nEverything we did with logarithms, we can do with the common logarithm. For example, we can\n\n- convert between exponential and logarithm form,\n\n- evaluate common logarithm expressions, and\n\n- calculate specific values of $\\log a$ using a calculator.\n\nFinally, note that most calculators have a button for the common logarithm. This is usually denoted $\\log_{10}$ or simply $\\log.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186084455 1064 "We have learned how to solve quadratic equations by factoring. Just like quadratic equations, some (but not all) cubic equations can also be solved by factoring.\n\nTo solve a cubic equation by factoring, we just need to follow three steps:\n\n1. Write the equation with $0$ on one side\n2. Factor the expression on the other side\n3. Set each factor equal to $0$ and solve\n\nTo factor the cubic, we can often use the method of grouping.\n\n**Note:** The cubic equations in the following examples can all be factored, but keep in mind that most cubic equations cannot be!" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186084455 1064 "We have learned how to solve quadratic equations by factoring. Just like quadratic equations, some (but not all) cubic equations can also be solved by factoring.\n\nTo solve a cubic equation by factoring, we just need to follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Write the equation with $0$ on one side\n\n2\\. Factor the expression on the other side\n\n3\\. Set each factor equal to $0$ and solve\n\nTo factor the cubic, we can often use the method of grouping.\n\n**Note:** The cubic equations in the following examples can all be factored, but keep in mind that most cubic equations cannot be!" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186092290 1038 17592186520502 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186092290 1038 17592186520508 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186092291 1038 17592186520483 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186092291 1038 17592186520485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186092293 1038 17592186520475 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186096842 1038 17592186520487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186096842 1038 17592186520516 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186096843 1038 17592186520457 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186096843 1038 17592186520473 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186096843 1038 17592186520484 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186100059 1078 17592186100062 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186100825 1038 17592186520481 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186100825 1038 17592186520488 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186100826 1038 17592186520460 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186100930 1038 17592186520497 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186101483 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186101483 1071 "![](images/17/174178e1d4c527bb81ea3ba726e5ef753b40dd85b452d65faf05590e7a387a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$?" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186101483 1071 "![](images/17/174178e1d4c527bb81ea3ba726e5ef753b40dd85b452d65faf05590e7a387a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186101483 1074 "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to $0$) when we pass through $x=4$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=4$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/877fcf017ab91010648de9a375e535ec7697841c8e317196c5759df6aca32e19.png)" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186123098 1075 17592186123099 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186123098 1075 17592186123100 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186123098 1075 17592186123101 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186123098 1075 17592186123102 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186123098 1075 17592186123103 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186123098 1075 17592186520031 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186123098 1075 17592186520042 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186123098 1075 17592186520058 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186123098 1075 17592186520088 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186123098 1075 17592186520365 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186123098 1078 17592186520042 13194139535589 17592