gned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186162666 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$, $Σ$, and $V$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the second column of $U$?" 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186162666 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186163340 1064 "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, the **conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} =\\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} x \\, f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)\\;\\textrm dx\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y$.\n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X]$. However, we now use the conditional PDF $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)$ instead of the marginal PDF $f_X(x)$ in our integral.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a particular value of $y$, the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, i.e., $\\textrm{E}\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$ *depends* on $y$, we have that $\\textrm{E}\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$ gives us a *function* of $y$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm E[X]$ for all $y$.\n- We sometimes use the notation $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$ to denote $E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n- We have an analogous definition for $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix}$:\n$\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} =\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} y \\, f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)\\;\\textrm dy$,\nwhere $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X$." 13194139535589 17592186045417 false] [17592186163340 1064 "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the **conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X |Y = y] =\\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} x \\, f_{X|Y}(x|y)\\;\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y.$ \n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X].$ However, we now use the conditional PDF $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ instead of the marginal PDF $f_X(x)$ in our integral.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a particular value of $y,$ the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, i.e., $\\textrm{E}[X |Y = y]$ *depends* on $y,$ we have that $\\textrm{E}[X |Y = y]$ gives us a *function* of $y.$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then 