true] [17592186520444 1075 17592186520352 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520444 1076 17592186045493 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520444 1077 "selection" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520444 1078 17592186520352 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520444 1079 #uuid "fd6a3276-afa5-552e-8370-0df3289262b0" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520445 1038 17592186520504 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520445 1039 "Comparing Two Events by Likelihood" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520445 1041 17592186520465 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520445 1042 #uuid "05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520446 1068 17592186520378 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520446 1069 "q-79509" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520446 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520446 1071 "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $120,\\,80,\\,30,\\,…$\n\nII. $-243,\\,27,\\,-3,\\,…$\n\nIII. $225,\\,45,\\,9,\\,…$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520446 1072 #uuid "011f3c60-0a40-5c48-8e42-7ae45f302869" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520446 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{80}{120}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{30}{80}=\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{27}{(-243)}=-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-3)}{27}=-\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is also geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{45}{225}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{9}{45}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences II and III could be geometric sequences." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520447 1068 17592186520373 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520447 1069 "q-101950" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520447 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520447 1071 "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=1$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520447 1072 #uuid "01c94ce2-04ad-5708-8ba5-8bfca9809325" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520447 1074 "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5(1+h)^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5+10h+5{h}^{2}-5}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10h+5{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(10+5h) \\\\  & =10+(0) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5({x}^{2}-1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}5(x+1) \\\\  & =5(1+1) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520448 1068 17592186520371 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520448 1069 "q-78745" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520448 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520448 1071 "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, find the value of $f(x)=3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x)$ at $x=3.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520448 1072 #uuid "0714c123-409e-586b-bfd3-01ab5d4541d7" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520448 1074 "First, we replace $x=3$ and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2\\cdot 3)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required value to $3$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $6$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =3\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(15) \\\\  & ≈3(0.778\\,151)-2(1.176\\,091) \\\\  & =2.334\\,453-2.352\\,182 \\\\  & =-0.017\\,729 \\\\  & ≈-0.018\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520449 1068 17592186520427 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520449 1069 "q-146238" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520449 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520449 1071 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,1.5)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1+2{y}^{2})}{5}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1.5]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1.5)$ is shown above." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520449 1072 #uuid "0729c3e7-6c49-5561-ba72-ac80cc555328" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520449 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1.5] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1.5)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3(1+2{y}^{2})}{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520450 1068 17592186520440 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520450 1069 "q-66630" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520450 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520450 1071 "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{10})=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520450 1072 #uuid "091b6406-ea9f-5edd-85ff-748cd7849985" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520450 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[{}^{3}]{10}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10}={10}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{10})=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1/3})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1/3})=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520451 1068 17592186520439 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520451 1069 "q-146337" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520451 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520451 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+{y}^{2})}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+1}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520451 1072 #uuid "13aa670b-3e27-50cc-b24b-f562a4443719" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520451 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x+{y}^{2}}{8})}{(\\frac{3x+1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+{y}^{2})}{3x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{3\\cdot 1+1}=\\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520452 1068 17592186520411 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520452 1069 "q-146517" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520452 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520452 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6(x+y)}{5}, & x\\le y\\le 2x,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520452 1072 #uuid "200b9c27-2539-5e86-b17a-fa5314a06310" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520452 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6(x+y)}{5})}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+y)}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\le y\\le 2x,\\,0<x<1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{x}^{2x}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{2(x+y)}{5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{x}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}[(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3})-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{23{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{23x\\cdot \\cancel{({x}^{2})}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{23x}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520453 1068 17592186520403 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520453 1069 "q-146345" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520453 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520453 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520453 1072 #uuid "28a2968a-07fa-5df6-a93c-3d505caa4a83" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520453 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12xy}{6{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|1\\,)=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520454 1068 17592186520399 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520454 1069 "q-114119" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520454 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520454 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation ${x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3x-15=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520454 1072 #uuid "2a147b6b-b927-5c0b-b768-701655ad01fc" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520454 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3x-15 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})+(3x-15) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-5)+3(x-5) & = \\\\ (x-5)\\cdot {x}^{2}+(x-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x-5)({x}^{2}+3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x-5)({x}^{2}+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $x=5$ is the only real solution." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520455 1068 17592186520430 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520455 1069 "q-95516" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520455 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520455 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(-2x),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520455 1072 #uuid "2bf70c41-d3e4-5066-92f9-b0265e8edce1" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520455 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(-2x)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|-2x|\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}}}\\cdot (-2x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2|x|\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520456 1068 17592186520395 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520456 1069 "q-109799" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520456 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520456 1071 "Given that $8$, $6x$, and $10x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520456 1072 #uuid "2e89be9b-05fd-570e-aab3-708af6042f1b" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520456 1074 "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=8,\\,{a}_{2}=6x,\\,{a}_{3}=10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{8}=\\frac{3x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{10x}{6x}=\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{4} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{20}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520457 1068 17592186520397 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520457 1069 "q-71952" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520457 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520457 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520457 1072 #uuid "321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520457 1074 "Let $g(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520458 1068 17592186520417 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520458 1069 "q-146188" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520458 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520458 1071 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2+2y}{3}, & \\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520458 1072 #uuid "33de5000-925c-5b02-ae4e-01b5310d9ba9" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520458 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2+2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520459 1068 17592186520372 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520459 1069 "q-114146" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520459 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520459 1071 "Solve ${n}^{3}+4{n}^{2}-9n-36=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520459 1072 #uuid "34ca4779-2bf7-5602-9812-9d6d9723327c" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520459 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+4{n}^{2}-9n-36 & = \\\\ ({n}^{3}+4{n}^{2})-(9n+36) & = \\\\ {n}^{2}(n+4)-9(n+4) & = \\\\ (n+4)\\cdot {n}^{2}-(n+4)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (n+4)({n}^{2}-9) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(n+4)({n}^{2}-9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(n+4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+4 & =0 \\\\ n & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({n}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =9 \\\\ n & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $n=-4,\\,\\pm 3.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520460 1068 17592186520436 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520460 1069 "q-114759" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520460 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520460 1071 "![](images/67/67cfcaae560edcf2fda8c69770088113b6035b46c9951f67ef81f13b8ea9615e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520460 1072 #uuid "3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520460 1074 "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ \n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are positive and negative, respectively.  \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7791dde88c3f8500047c0ebeb4553055d76a98c0c46f903816f41c1506d6a6a.png)" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520461 1068 17592186520437 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520461 1069 "q-114335" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520461 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520461 1071 "Solve $3{t}^{3}-15{t}^{2}-9t+45=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520461 1072 #uuid "3e7651d9-4306-5281-ab2c-60e5fbcc4dde" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520461 1074 "Since we have an overall common factor of $3$, we start by dividing our equation through by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15 & = \\\\ ({t}^{3}-5{t}^{2})-(3t-15) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-5)-3(t-5) & = \\\\ (t-5)\\cdot {t}^{2}-(t-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (t-5)({t}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(t-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({t}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $5$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520462 1068 17592186520416 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520462 1069 "q-146433" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520462 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520462 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0<x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}24{x}^{2}, & 0<x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520462 1072 #uuid "4a9126d5-9f56-54dd-b358-d393eac16b93" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520462 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{24{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0<x<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{16}{2}-0] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520463 1068 17592186520406 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520463 1069 "q-146254" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520463 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520463 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-x-y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}-y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520463 1072 #uuid "4aa42e03-e8d1-5333-8d07-d9074e34a91f" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520463 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-x-y)}{(\\frac{3}{2}-y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2x-2y}{3-2y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{4-2x-2\\cdot 1}{3-2\\cdot 1}=\\frac{4-2x-2}{3-2}=2-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2-2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520464 1068 17592186520384 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520464 1069 "q-146520" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520464 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520464 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{5}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y+2}{5}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ for $0<y<1.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520464 1072 #uuid "4c1f6156-411f-54e4-bb68-bbf84af25120" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520464 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{5})}{(\\frac{6y+2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{6y+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\,\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{6y+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{y}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}\\cdot \\frac{1+2y}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2(3y+1)}\\cdot \\frac{(1+2y)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+2y)}{4(3y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520465 1069 "e-24942" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520465 1071 "A scientist records how long it takes for a chemical reaction to complete. The experiment is conducted $40$ times. The table below shows the results.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<10$ | $6$ |\n| $10-20$ | $14$ |\n| $20-30$ | $8$ |\n| $30-40$ | $7$ |\n| $>40$ | $5$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{unlikely}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{as likely as not}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{less likely than}}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520465 1072 #uuid "4ced75f7-e2c9-50f2-b2cf-114222fc960d" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520465 1074 "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took more than $30$ seconds $(7+5=12)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/41/41ae3b0ee5b77a5967ee949abfd5dc5327fc840e9061cecf1dfd52728405415f.svg)\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0 < \\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{unlikely}}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took less than $20$ seconds $(6+14=20)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/19/194542c8095442fe631a733e681d43b025ac5b5f7f478f7b3c541e3c51718c69.svg)\n\nSince the probability is $\\dfrac12,$ this event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{as likely as not}}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is less than the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{less likely than}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520466 1068 17592186520435 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520466 1069 "q-146538" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520466 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520466 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x+2y)}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+4y}{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520466 1072 #uuid "5142c769-0ab4-5c62-90f2-d5f937ec0c66" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520466 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2(x+2y)}{3})}{(\\frac{1+4y}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\,\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+2yx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+y{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}+y-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}\\cdot \\frac{1+3y}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+3y)}{3(1+4y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520467 1068 17592186520433 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520467 1069 "q-341509" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520467 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520467 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=4\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{x}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520467 1072 #uuid "56abd260-e88d-5e15-85ad-d09d0617c5fa" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520467 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[4\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\sqrt[4]{x})] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}\\sqrt{1-{(\\sqrt[4]{x})}^{2}}}]\\cdot {(\\sqrt[4]{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[4]{x}\\sqrt{1-\\sqrt{x}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-\\sqrt{x}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520468 1068 17592186520444 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520468 1069 "q-95522" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520468 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520468 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡5x,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520468 1072 #uuid "56f1a503-7979-5afa-a86c-36de0338e547" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520468 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡5x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡5x) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{(5x)\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}})\\cdot (5x)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5x\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520469 1068 17592186520432 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520469 1069 "q-99890" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520469 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520469 1071 "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, $\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}{3\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)}=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520469 1072 #uuid "5a64a8e4-76a1-5b39-9c41-0b568b095115" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520469 1074 "We compute the required expression to $3$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $6$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}{3\\operatorname{log}⁡(11)} & ≈\\frac{0.903\\,090}{3(1.041\\,393)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.903\\,090}{3.124\\,179} \\\\  & ≈0.289\\,065 \\\\  & ≈0.289\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520470 1068 17592186520374 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520470 1069 "q-52474" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520470 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520470 1071 "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $-1,\\,-6,\\,-36,\\,…$\n\nII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$\n\nIII. $2,\\,7,\\,12,\\,…$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520470 1072 #uuid "5bf4971e-04f4-56ea-b8f7-ec174707b886" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520470 1074 "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-6)}{(-1)}=6 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-36)}{(-6)}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is also not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{12}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I could be a geometric sequence." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520471 1068 17592186520379 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520471 1069 "q-250656" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520471 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520471 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n243,\\,-162,\\,108,\\,-72,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of this geometric sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520471 1072 #uuid "5d70384a-18d5-5ae8-aba3-5e0332c40c5f" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520471 1074 "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-162)}{243}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520472 1068 17592186520368 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520472 1069 "q-104994" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520472 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520472 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(3,-3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(3,-3)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum of $f$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520472 1072 #uuid "60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520472 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520473 1068 17592186520387 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520473 1069 "q-107296" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520473 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520473 1071 "Given that $f(x)={(1-3x)}^{8},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520473 1072 #uuid "73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520473 1074 "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{8}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{8})^{′}=8{g}^{7}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =8{g}^{7}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-24(1-3x)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520474 1068 17592186520381 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520474 1069 "q-146397" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520474 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520474 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520474 1072 #uuid "7abac60c-426c-5228-9bff-bdb217864577" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520474 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{4})}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0<x<1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}[4-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520475 1068 17592186520376 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520475 1069 "q-125986" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520475 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520475 1071 "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1)$ and $x\\ge -1.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520475 1072 #uuid "7e900458-4a34-53b2-8b6d-fd38c3b57268" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520475 1074 "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x+1).$ Since $x\\ge -1,$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(y+1),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge -1.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{y+1}+{e}^{-(y+1)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{y+1}+{e}^{-(y+1)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{y+1} & ={e}^{2(y+1)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(y+1)}-2x{e}^{y+1}+1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{y+1})}^{2}-2x({e}^{y+1})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{y+1}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since $y+1\\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be non-negative. This requires its argument to be greater than or equal to $1$ for $x\\ge 1$, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})-1.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520476 1068 17592186520375 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520476 1069 "q-47887" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520476 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520476 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}$, the instantaneous rate of change ${f}^{′}(-1)$ can be computed using the limit of a difference quotient. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(-1).$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520476 1072 #uuid "7fa38cf7-46e9-5719-8fa2-6633394b103a" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520476 1074 "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1-2h+{h}^{2}-1}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-2+h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-2+h) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520477 1068 17592186520434 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520477 1069 "q-68204" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520477 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520477 1071 "![](images/1a/1a5313995e9740a61b455a9c368aca6223a80c8bbea659f06ec2a63c8260747b.png)\n\nKaren decides to walk from her workplace to her house. There is a proportional relationship between the distance $x$ (in kilometers) she covers and the time $y$ (in hours) it takes her to cover that distance.\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the graph above?" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520477 1072 #uuid "817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520477 1074 "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,1).$\n\n![](images/be/bec01f9dc01b340075f09dd459571fe8199116f18a6ab912eb958c7a6f9ee4f2.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520478 1068 17592186520392 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520478 1069 "q-341473" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520478 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520478 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4}$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520478 1072 #uuid "8198f6db-6c5f-5983-9cb0-0bd0190f9596" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520478 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|{x}^{2}+4|\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{2}+4)}^{2}}}\\cdot {({x}^{2}+4)}^{′}-\\frac{{({x}^{2}+4)}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+4)\\sqrt{1+({x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16)}}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+4)\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+17}}-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+4}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+17}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520479 1068 17592186520388 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520479 1069 "q-341514" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520479 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520479 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520479 1072 #uuid "83895ad6-7f6a-50d5-a3b6-af8475bcabcc" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520479 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}}]\\cdot {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520480 1068 17592186520386 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520480 1069 "q-341483" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520480 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520480 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520480 1072 #uuid "84e76c93-4252-53d8-b14a-d598aa6e8e08" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520480 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{|{sin}^{2}⁡x|\\sqrt{1+{({sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{2}}}]\\cdot {({sin}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{4}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520481 1068 17592186520405 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520481 1069 "q-20254" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520481 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520481 1071 "![](images/42/42e163dd119aaea37d2a373310b408a409023400a6f84ff520c464c6acb153b9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520481 1072 #uuid "862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520481 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0.3)$ | $0.3$ | $(0.3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈-0.3.$ So ${f}^{″}(-0.3)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(-0.3)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈-0.3,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png)" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520482 1068 17592186520390 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520482 1069 "q-47870" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520482 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520482 1071 "Given that $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$, what is ${f}^{′}(2)$ according to the definition?" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520482 1072 #uuid "864d30c1-9b9e-5747-9ebf-06a4c172035c" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520482 1074 "The definition of ${f}^{′}(2)$ is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}).\n$$\n\nLet $a=2.$ Then, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}).\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520483 1068 17592186520423 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520483 1069 "q-125855" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520483 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520483 1071 "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{15}{17})=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520483 1072 #uuid "8ddac48b-cef1-5d70-9862-741511be4eb3" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520483 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{15}{17}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{15}{17})}{1-(-\\frac{15}{17})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{15}{17}}{1+\\frac{15}{17}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2}{15}}{\\frac{32}{15}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{-4} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520484 1068 17592186520410 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520484 1069 "q-314207" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520484 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520484 1071 "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520484 1072 #uuid "930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520484 1074 "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})(\\,4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520485 1068 17592186520370 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520485 1069 "q-125859" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520485 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520485 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $t>-\\frac{1}{2},$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{t}{t+1})?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520485 1072 #uuid "9750db93-431a-52f4-a35e-36e894133bed" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520485 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{t}{t+1}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{t}{t+1}}{1-\\frac{t}{t+1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{t+1+t}{t+1}}{\\frac{t+1-t}{t+1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2t+1}{t+1}}{\\frac{1}{t+1}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520486 1068 17592186520420 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520486 1069 "q-163108" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520486 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520486 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{e}^{y}),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax+by-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+cxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520486 1072 #uuid "983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520486 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520487 1068 17592186520404 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520487 1069 "q-107303" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520487 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520487 1071 "Given that $y=\\sqrt{2x-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520487 1072 #uuid "98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520487 1074 "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520488 1068 17592186520389 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520488 1069 "q-114627" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520488 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520488 1071 "![](images/fe/fe369706a9e211bfc5265041bbc5d2ec0fab69eb205c23111f548785a2f88708.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520488 1072 #uuid "a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520488 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ So ${f}^{″}(0)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(0)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈0,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f5a0aa83f9348cfae1bf2ef472a00e1da51137d03284bc78eea67d929aaa80e.png)" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520489 1068 17592186520421 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520489 1069 "q-146177" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520489 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520489 1071 "$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,3)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}, & \\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=3]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)$ is shown above." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520489 1072 #uuid "a3d747d1-36eb-5ac7-aecf-ff0858227dfd" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520489 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=3] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{32}-\\frac{0}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520490 1068 17592186520419 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520490 1069 "q-101241" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520490 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520490 1071 "Given that $50$, $5x$, and $12.5x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520490 1072 #uuid "aefd99db-cf1d-5552-bc9d-e8816ab40f33" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520490 1074 "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=50,\\,{a}_{2}=5x,\\,{a}_{3}=12.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5x}{50}=\\frac{x}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12.5x}{5x}=2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5 & =\\frac{x}{10} \\\\ 25 & =x \\\\ x & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520491 1068 17592186520429 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520491 1069 "q-146253" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520491 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520491 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3y}{12}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520491 1072 #uuid "b25c780b-4145-5bfa-8e04-0856e9d67d3a" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520491 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x+3y}{12})}{(\\frac{y+1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3y}{3y+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{2x+3\\cdot 1}{3\\cdot 1+3}=\\frac{2x+3}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{6}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}((\\frac{16}{3}+6)-(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520492 1068 17592186520443 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520492 1069 "q-146360" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520492 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520492 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0<x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520492 1072 #uuid "b86ab1d5-8833-598a-9757-b29ccf7f9acc" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520492 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8xy}{4{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0<x\\le 1,\\,0<y\\le x.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|1\\,)=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520493 1068 17592186520428 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520493 1069 "q-114198" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520493 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520493 1071 "Solve $36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2}-16d+36=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520493 1072 #uuid "bb562da5-ef3b-592a-bfc0-4d26f88b1f50" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520493 1074 "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2}-16d+36 & = \\\\ (36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2})-(16d-36) & = \\\\ 9{d}^{2}(4d-9)-4(4d-9) & = \\\\ (4d-9)\\cdot 9{d}^{2}-(4d-9)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ (4d-9)(9{d}^{2}-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(4d-9)(9{d}^{2}-4)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(4d-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4d-9 & =0 \\\\ 4d & =9 \\\\ d & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{d}^{2}-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{d}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 9{d}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $d=\\frac{9}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520494 1068 17592186520394 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520494 1069 "q-114207" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520494 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520494 1071 "Solve $-2{r}^{3}+12{r}^{2}+3r-18=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520494 1072 #uuid "bbddec8f-0415-5a7f-a473-3ac96a89f653" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520494 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2}-3r+18=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2}-3r+18 & = \\\\ (2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2})+(-3r+18) & = \\\\ 2{r}^{2}(r-6)-3(r-6) & = \\\\ (2{r}^{2}-3)(r-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2{r}^{2}-3)(r-6)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{r}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $r=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(r-6)$ equal to $0,$ we get $r=6.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $r=6,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520495 1068 17592186520391 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520495 1069 "q-250632" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520495 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520495 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n25,\\,-5,\\,1,\\,-\\frac{1}{5},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of this geometric sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520495 1072 #uuid "bfa27842-a6ff-52d4-867b-7142afc0e5fe" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520495 1074 "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-5)}{25}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520496 1068 17592186520402 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520496 1069 "q-94606" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520496 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520496 1071 "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=(z-2)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520496 1072 #uuid "c06702a9-7f9f-5ebf-bbd9-2e359cfd7640" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520496 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{8} & =(0-2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{16}{8} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2}+2 & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-6 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =6\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(4,0,0)$ and $(8,0,0).$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520497 1068 17592186520415 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520497 1069 "q-114644" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520497 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520497 1071 "![](images/53/53693867d697a4aaf7bedd2267ac2e77087b01a270f44ce68e9a4d303b81a5ad.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520497 1072 #uuid "c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520497 1074 "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3b558331bf2e816cf0df6d756f55686aeb06f6ec9ce496019bd06d14684b32.png)" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520498 1068 17592186520426 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520498 1069 "q-108088" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520498 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520498 1071 "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt[5]{10})=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520498 1072 #uuid "d37627d8-2221-5b06-8378-80d838ec2dc0" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520498 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[5]{10}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[5]{10}={10}^{1/5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt[5]{10})=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1/5})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1/5})=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520499 1068 17592186520369 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520499 1069 "q-163005" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520499 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520499 1071 "![](images/4d/4df58d2f35cc69e8a567a65bc64f24e5da8820e1be5d229979b08fe2c3d1cd8f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in months) since Mary's baby was born and the number $y$ of centimeters he has grown. What is the baby's unit rate of growth?" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520499 1072 #uuid "d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520499 1074 "The baby's unit rate of growth in centimeters per month is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(2,5).$\n\n![](images/5b/5bbc6c5b4188c5db894ceebe7d347c901afb428d5d102864a90218ef53420a60.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary's baby's unit rate of growth is $\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520500 1068 17592186520396 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520500 1069 "q-313233" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520500 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520500 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=3\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(2x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{|x|}$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520500 1072 #uuid "d8788c2a-c953-5587-a4b0-071fdea7bf08" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520500 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(3\\,\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|2x|\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}}})\\cdot (2x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2|x|\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{|x|}\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520501 1068 17592186520380 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520501 1069 "q-95513" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520501 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520501 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡\\sqrt{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520501 1072 #uuid "da5d7294-c739-5f77-ae4a-e6ad7eaa5e42" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520501 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{|\\sqrt{x}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{x})^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2x\\sqrt{1+x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520502 1068 17592186520442 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520502 1069 "q-300604" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520502 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520502 1071 "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3.$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520502 1072 #uuid "da7e5a10-376b-5a22-a310-bd1bf7fa24cf" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520502 1074 "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{9-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3+\\sqrt{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520503 1068 17592186520400 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520503 1069 "q-146180" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520503 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520503 1071 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{12}, & \\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520503 1072 #uuid "db6164d4-0ab9-5b8d-a944-50886d9aea3b" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520503 1074 "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\cdot \\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{12}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(y+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(99-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1068 17592186520413 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1068 17592186520422 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1068 17592186520441 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1069 "q-360663" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1071 "Sarah conducts an experiment $40$ times to see how quickly a machine sorts a bin of parts. The table below shows the results of her experiments.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<20$ | $6$ |\n| $20-30$ | $16$ |\n| $30-40$ | $10$ |\n| $40-50$ | $5$ |\n| $>50$ | $3$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$A machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n{{field-3}}\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1072 #uuid "e4735ca8-e709-5697-8aaf-f9b52ab3104d" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520504 1074 "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds $(6+16=22)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a relatively large number, closer to $1.$ Since\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}<\\frac{22}{40}<1,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{likely}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a machine sorted a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds $(10+5+3=18)$ by the total number of times Sarah conducted the experiment $(40).$ This gives\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{18}{40}.\n$$\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0<\\frac{18}{40}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\text{unlikely}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is *greater than* the experimental probability of a machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds:\n\n$$\nP(\\text{sorts in }<30\\text{ seconds})=\\frac{22}{40}>\\frac{18}{40}=P(\\text{sorts in }>30\\text{ seconds})\n$$\n\nTherefore, a machine sorting a bin of parts in less than $30$ seconds is\n$\\text{more likely than}$\na machine sorting a bin of parts in more than $30$ seconds." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520505 1068 17592186520409 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520505 1069 "q-108087" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520505 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520505 1071 "Find the value of $2\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t)$ at $t=10.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520505 1072 #uuid "e5c7ea95-c351-53b5-8952-0f4a790122f8" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520505 1074 "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=3$ and $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2\\cdot 3-2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520506 1068 17592186520425 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520506 1069 "q-341507" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520506 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520506 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x}),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520506 1072 #uuid "e713a272-c241-525b-b381-4fe5ba553286" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520506 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic secant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsech}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}\\sqrt{1-{(\\sqrt[3]{x})}^{2}}}\\cdot {(\\sqrt[3]{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}\\sqrt{1-\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1-\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520507 1068 17592186520385 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520507 1069 "q-72088" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520507 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520507 1071 "Solve $y$ if ${y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-2y-10=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520507 1072 #uuid "e76e6bf7-d54d-5018-81b2-f60c46589b4a" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520507 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-2y-10 & = \\\\ ({y}^{3}+5{y}^{2})-(2y+10) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}(y+5)-2(y+5) & = \\\\ (y+5)\\cdot {y}^{2}-(y+5)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (y+5)({y}^{2}-2) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(y+5)({y}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(y+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5 & =0 \\\\ y & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({y}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-5,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520508 1068 17592186520401 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520508 1069 "q-125854" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520508 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520508 1071 "$$\n\nGiven that $y\\ge 1,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\sqrt{y}?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520508 1072 #uuid "e77b2e22-83c2-5884-b6a1-4a4bd43f7297" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520508 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520509 1068 17592186520412 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520509 1069 "q-96323" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520509 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520509 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520509 1072 #uuid "eeba80ad-bee9-5269-baef-fbd3bd2d6cda" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520509 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}73 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-81I & =[\\begin{aligned}73 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 9\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -24 \\\\ -24 & -72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}73 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}72 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520510 1068 17592186520393 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520510 1069 "q-163105" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520510 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520510 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y+4},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520510 1072 #uuid "f0b66b9d-4d88-5de4-a9f2-e4468c8b8ed2" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520510 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot x+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{32})xy+(-\\frac{1}{64}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{32})+(-\\frac{1}{64})=\\frac{13}{64}.\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520511 1068 17592186520438 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520511 1069 "q-66628" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520511 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520511 1071 "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.01)=$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520511 1072 #uuid "f698077a-723c-5eb7-8769-9b9cd991b10a" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520511 1074 "First, we rewrite $0.01$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.01={10}^{-2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.01)=\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{-2})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{-2})=-2\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520512 1068 17592186520408 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520512 1069 "q-114373" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520512 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520512 1071 "Solve $30{n}^{3}+15{n}^{2}-20n-10=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520512 1072 #uuid "f6abbc6d-e05c-5882-80a0-628ebefcab1b" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520512 1074 "Since we have an overall common factor of $5$, we start by dividing our equation through by $5.$ This gives \n\n$$\n6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}-4n-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}-4n-2 & = \\\\ (6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2})-(4n+2) & = \\\\ 3{n}^{2}(2n+1)-2(2n+1) & = \\\\ (2n+1)\\cdot 3{n}^{2}-(2n+1)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (2n+1)(3{n}^{2}-2) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2n+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+1 & =0 \\\\ n & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3{n}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{n}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{n}^{2} & =2 \\\\ n & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520513 1068 17592186520424 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520513 1069 "q-66546" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520513 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520513 1071 "What exponential equation is equivalent to the logarithmic equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=\\frac{1}{2}?$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520513 1072 #uuid "f7974a75-c042-5467-9fad-57099eb48349" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520513 1074 "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={10}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=\\frac{1}{2}$ with $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x,$ we get that $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${10}^{1/2}=a.$\n\nFinally, since ${10}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{10},$ the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{10}=a.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520514 1068 17592186520398 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520514 1069 "q-105213" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520514 1070 17592186045487 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520514 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,1)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520514 1072 #uuid "f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520514 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=det({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520515 1068 17592186520431 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520515 1069 "q-14112" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520515 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520515 1071 "Solve ${b}^{3}-2{b}^{2}-6b+12=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520515 1072 #uuid "f84ff77e-8bf5-5075-9613-766131045ace" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520515 1074 "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{3}-2{b}^{2}-6b+12 & = \\\\ ({b}^{3}-2{b}^{2})-(6b-12) & = \\\\ {b}^{2}(b-2)-6(b-2) & = \\\\ (b-2)\\cdot {b}^{2}-(b-2)\\cdot 6 & = \\\\ (b-2)({b}^{2}-6) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(b-2)({b}^{2}-6)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(b-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({b}^{2}-6)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {b}^{2} & =6 \\\\ b & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $b=2,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520516 1068 17592186520382 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520516 1069 "q-314192" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520516 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520516 1071 "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+1}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520516 1072 #uuid "fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520516 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(0)+1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520517 1068 17592186520377 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520517 1069 "q-14100" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520517 1070 17592186045486 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520517 1071 "Solve $20{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-32y-16=0.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520517 1072 #uuid "faa06da2-05be-56dd-9e44-883b8d4527f9" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520517 1074 "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8 & = \\\\ (10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2})-(16y+8) & = \\\\ 5{y}^{2}(2y+1)-8(2y+1) & = \\\\ (2y+1)\\cdot 5{y}^{2}-(2y+1)\\cdot 8 & = \\\\ (2y+1)(5{y}^{2}-8) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2y+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(5{y}^{2}-8)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520518 1068 17592186520418 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520518 1069 "q-250679" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520518 1070 17592186045485 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520518 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe sixth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520518 1072 #uuid "feb40554-9f62-5369-873c-1a4212bcc47c" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520518 1074 "![](images/64/64ab493abc4e32afc36676b20cf0e8898c4d0ba07c086280e426890c7bdfb70a.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{10}{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $5.$ \n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $6$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/32/32f60b73a942c4e792f3629b5f4824f1c639a25e03491a36f81679d126bcd685.png)\n\nTherefore, the $6$th term is $6250.$" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520519 1062 #uuid "01cfa563-e8bf-5304-95ca-8bb9735ba5e0" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520519 1063 17592186512877 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520520 1062 #uuid "05b93622-e0e7-508b-9fee-2ccaa8ca47d3" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520520 1063 17592186234280 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520521 1062 #uuid "0628f9ee-2554-5fa8-8aa0-10a3d900487f" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520521 1063 17592186512917 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520522 1062 #uuid "0d2cb728-c445-559a-a95d-812914b99b3c" 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [17592186520522 1063 17592186520504 13194139535589 17592186045417 true] [175921865205